The lab was supposed to be empty. Security protocols demanded a twenty-four hour cooldown period after the day's experiments, but Johnny had never been one for rules that applied to other people.\
"Increase the spin rate to 5,000 RPM," he whispered into the control room microphone, though the nearest guards were three corridors away. "And bring the temporal compensator online."\
Through the observation window, the Gyges Disc rotated on its axis, casting rainbow patterns across the chamber walls. Chilo stood beside him, similarly unconcerned about protocol violations.\
"The temporal calibration seems stable at this speed," Chilo murmured. "Perhaps faster?"\
Johnny's fingers twitched at the controls. "Quark said anything above 8,000 RPM requires additional safety measures we haven't implemented yet."\
"Dr Quark is cautious by nature." Chilo's voice was silk over steel. "But caution rarely yields breakthrough discoveries, does it?"\
In the lower levels of Sublime Point, Sarah Jacobson jolted awake, her sheets drenched in sweat. The patterns\'97those horrible, mesmerizing patterns\'97danced behind her eyelids. She'd seen them again: concentric circles collapsing inward, energy ribbons that seemed to twist reality itself.\
Racing to her bathroom, she splashed cold water on her face. The nightmares were getting worse. Ever since arriving at Sublime Point, they'd intensified. It was as if something here was calling to her, resonating with whatever the pregnancy had awakened within her.\
A security alert pinged on her nightstand tablet. Someone was in the restricted lab areas. She grabbed her robe and headed for the elevators.\
Back in the control room, Johnny had pushed the disc to 6,000 RPM. The patterns etched into the chamber walls from the previous accident began to glow faintly.\
"Remarkable," Chilo breathed. "The energy signature is harmonizing with the existing damage patterns. As if the disc remembers its own influence."\
"Increase to 7,000," Johnny ordered, adrenaline overriding his earlier caution.\
The building's structure began to emit a low-frequency hum. In the bunker, gravity itself seemed to fluctuate\'97lab equipment lifted momentarily off benches before crashing back down.\
The door to the control room suddenly burst open. Chip Stockwell stood in the doorway, still in his pajamas but clutching his security badge. "What the hell are you doing?"\
"Security breach!" Johnny moved to cut the power, but Chilo's hand shot out, gripping his wrist.\
"Let's see what happens at 8,000," Chilo suggested calmly.\
"Are you insane?" Chip rushed forward. "Look at the temporal readings\'97you're creating localized time distortions. At this rate, you'll tear a hole in spacetime itself!"\
"Tear a hole... or open a door?" Chilo mused.\
Sarah arrived just as the building began to shake in earnest. Through the observation window, she saw the disc spinning so fast it appeared as a solid sphere of light. The patterns on the walls now pulsed in perfect synchronization with the rhythm of her nightmares.\
"Johnny, stop!" she commanded, her voice carrying an authority she hadn't possessed months ago. "Those patterns\'97I've seen them. They're a warning, not an invitation!"\
But in the bunker, reality had begun to bend. The corners of the room seemed to stretch, spatial dimensions becoming malleable. A technician's forgotten coffee mug aged twenty years in seconds, then reverted to pristine condition.\
"Temporal cycling," Chip reported grimly, studying his tablet. "We're creating localized temporal loops. At this frequency\'97"\
A crack appeared in the air itself\'97not in the glass, but in empty space between them and the observation window. Through it, they glimpsed flashes of other places, other times: the same lab in different configurations, possibly across various universes.\
"Shutdown protocol, now!" Sarah ordered.\
Johnny, finally recognizing genuine danger, slammed the emergency stop. The disc began to decelerate, but the crack in reality remained, slowly sealing itself like a healing wound.\
As the equipment powered down and the temporal anomalies subsided, the four of them stood in heavy silence. The patterns that had glowed on the walls gradually faded, leaving behind only the original blast damage\'97and Sarah's haunted expression as she recognized them from her dreams.\
"This isn't over," Chip said quietly. "We've barely scratched the surface of what that disc can do\'97and what it can do to us."\
Chilo said nothing, but a small smile played across his face as he noted how the crack had taken several minutes to fully close. Next time, they would need more power, more speed, more time.\
